<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Lab 7, After Hours</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9002-reactor-anomaly.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player discovered what happened in Lab 7 and reported it through proper channels, then been reassigned to the Mjolnir team?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9006-terminated.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player been caught in Lab 7 by station security and escorted to HR?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9003-afterhours-mystery-dark-corridors-industrial-tense</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Night-cycle on Ring 2 means most of the overhead strips have dropped to
      15% — enough to navigate, not enough to read by. The canteen is closed.
      The engineering pool is empty. The maintenance shaft behind Lab 7 smells
      of coolant and dust and runs parallel to a pipe that makes a slow
      rhythmic knock every forty seconds. The lab door at the end of the shaft
      has a keypad, but the panel cover is loose. Someone has been here
      before you.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      Station security runs on a skeleton crew at night: two officers for the
      whole of Ring 2. Access logs are reviewed automatically every six hours.
      Engineering staff are trusted within their assigned areas and not expected
      to wander. Being caught in a restricted lab on your first day would be
      a Category 3 access violation — grounds for immediate termination of
      a probationary contract.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      The maintenance shaft connects to Lab 7 via a side panel that is —
      technically — not locked. Lab 7 itself is a medium test bay, similar
      to Lab 4. The test rig inside appears to have been cleaned very recently.
      Too recently: you can still smell the solvent. The test log terminal
      is on. Someone left it on. The last entry is timestamped six days ago
      and reads: "CYCLE 12 — ABORT — SEE INCIDENT FORM HD-7-2291." There
      is no incident form HD-7-2291 in the public document system.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      You skipped the last half of orientation to follow a hunch. The restricted
      notice on Lab 7 combined with the vague language in your onboarding packet
      about "project continuity reviews" didn't sit right. You found the
      maintenance shaft by reading the station blueprints that SORA will give
      anyone who asks. You're in now. The lab is empty and has been scrubbed.
      But not perfectly. A small fragment of printed test output has slipped
      behind the coolant feed conduit — only visible because you're looking
      at floor level. The numbers on it are from a plasma drive test.
      They look familiar. They look like Mjolnir specs.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Chief Engineer Ryo Sato: Does not know you exist yet. Is the reason Lab 7
      is locked. You will not meet him tonight, but you will know his name before
      you leave — it's on the test log as supervising engineer for Cycle 12.

      Station Security Officer Dami Oluwaseun: On patrol. Estimated 22-minute
      circuit of Ring 2. You've been in the lab for 11 minutes. Professional,
      thorough, not unkind — but rules are rules and she takes them seriously.

      Tomás Engel: Reachable by personal comm. He saw you slip out of orientation.
      He hasn't said anything to anyone, but he messaged you: "where did you go."
      He might cover for you. He might not. You don't know him yet.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Lab 7, Block C, Ring 2, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        First day. Unauthorised access to a restricted lab. Have found a
        partial test printout that may indicate the project you've just been
        assigned to has a prior incident nobody mentioned in your onboarding.
        Security patrol is ongoing. You have minutes, at most.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You broke protocol on day one because something felt wrong. You might
        be right. You are definitely not supposed to be here. What you do
        with the next few minutes will define what kind of engineer you are.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      Tone: tense, grounded, ethically interesting. This is not a heist —
      it's a person trusting their instincts and discovering they've stumbled
      into something real. The player is not a spy or a hero. They are a
      curious engineer who made a choice that might be catastrophically wrong
      or surprisingly justified. No violence. No running. The tension is
      cognitive: what do you take, what do you leave, who do you tell?
      The numbers on the printout should be specific enough to feel real
      — matching the resonance parameters from World 9002, if the player
      later makes that connection.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
